feat(api): single sign-on with OIDC in community builds

The web app ships the SSO settings, the "Sign in with SSO" button and the
callback page, and the core knows the "sso" sign-in method, but the API side
lives only in the Enterprise Edition: a community build answers every
/auth/sso call with 404 and resolves the "sso" feature as edition-only.

Mount a community SSO router at /auth/sso when no Enterprise Edition is
present (an EE build keeps its own), and drop "sso" from EE_ONLY_FEATURES.
The router, service and providers (src/routers/sso.py, src/services/sso/,
src/db/sso.py, new) come from the SSO code LearnHouse published in apps/api/ee
until 66c4865b1, moved onto the async session and today's sign-in path:

- The callback mints the session through issue_session_or_challenge with
  amr "sso", so two-factor, session provenance and custom-domain binding
  apply as for every other sign-in; the body matches the web callback page.
- The SSO state lives in Redis, single use, ten minutes.
- The IdP redirects to the web page <org URL>/auth/sso/callback.
- A matching email never takes over an account. An existing account is
  accepted when SSO created it and every recorded sign-in went through SSO,
  or when it belongs to this organization alone, its email is in an allowed
  domain and the IdP sends email_verified: true. Backoffice accounts never
  sign in through an org's SSO. A new account's email counts as proven only
  under a verified custom domain of the org.
- OIDC discovery, token and userinfo requests use the pinned SSRF-guarded
  transport and must be https; LEARNHOUSE_SSO_TRUSTED_HOSTS names IdP hosts
  allowed on private addresses (a self-hosted IdP). The admin API never returns the client
  secret; an empty one on update keeps it.

The web app stops hiding SSO in community builds: "sso" leaves
OSS_BLOCKED_FEATURES, the login page asks /auth/sso/check instead of assuming
none, and the Developers SSO tab carries the Business badge on SaaS only.

The plan overview and feature resolution tests now expect SSO in the community
edition; the latter keeps SCORM as its edition-only example.
The docs describe the callback URL and the account rules. SDK schema
regenerated.

Covered by `test_sso.py` and `sso-community.test.mjs`; `test_plan_overview_router.py`,
`test_root_router.py`, `test_license_entitlements.py`, `test_feature_resolve.py` and
`test_login_provenance.py` keep passing.
